E2. Concrete Example: Fundamental Theorem of Finitely Generated Abelian Groups

The theorem states that any finitely generated Abelian group \(G\) can be broken down uniquely into a direct sum of infinite cyclic groups (\(\mathbb{Z}\)) and finite prime-power cyclic groups (\(\mathbb{Z}/p^k\mathbb{Z}\))—called elementary divisors:
\(G\cong \mathbb{Z}^{r}\oplus \mathbb{Z}/p_{1}^{k_{1}}\mathbb{Z}\oplus \mathbb{Z}/p_{2}^{k_{2}}\mathbb{Z}\oplus \cdots \oplus \mathbb{Z}/p_{m}^{k_{m}}\mathbb{Z}\)

Step-by-Step Concrete Example

Suppose we start with a group of size 72 built as a direct sum:
\(G=\mathbb{Z}/12\mathbb{Z}\oplus \mathbb{Z}/6\mathbb{Z}\)
(Here, \(12 \times 6 = 72\) elements, and there is no infinite \(\mathbb{Z}\) part, so the rank \(r = 0\).)
1. Factor Each Component into Prime Powers:
Just like factoring integers into primes, we factor \(12\) and \(6\):
	• \(12 = 2^2 \times 3^1 = 4 \times 3\)
	• \(6 = 2^1 \times 3^1 = 2 \times 3\)
2. Apply the Chinese Remainder Theorem:
Whenever two numbers \(a\) and \(b\) share no common factors (\(\gcd(a, b) = 1\)), the cyclic group \(\mathbb{Z}/ab\mathbb{Z}\) splits cleanly into \(\mathbb{Z}/a\mathbb{Z} \oplus \mathbb{Z}/b\mathbb{Z}\):
	• \(\mathbb{Z}/12\mathbb{Z} \cong \mathbb{Z}/4\mathbb{Z} \oplus \mathbb{Z}/3\mathbb{Z}\)
	• \(\mathbb{Z}/6\mathbb{Z} \cong \mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z} \oplus \mathbb{Z}/3\mathbb{Z}\)
(Note: \(\mathbb{Z}/4\mathbb{Z}\) cannot be split into \(\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z} \oplus \mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}\) because \(\gcd(2, 2) = 2 \neq 1\)—prime powers are the indivisible "atoms" of finite Abelian groups!)
3. Assemble the Unique Prime-Power Decomposition:
Putting the pieces together gives the elementary divisor form:
\(G\cong \mathbb{Z}/2^{2}\mathbb{Z}\oplus \mathbb{Z}/2^{1}\mathbb{Z}\oplus \mathbb{Z}/3^{1}\mathbb{Z}\oplus \mathbb{Z}/3^{1}\mathbb{Z}\)

Why This Matters (Comparing Distinct Groups of Order 72)

Using this theorem, we can immediately tell whether two messy groups are secretly identical or structurally different—even when both have 72 elements:
• Group A: \(\mathbb{Z}/12\mathbb{Z} \oplus \mathbb{Z}/6\mathbb{Z} \cong \mathbb{Z}/4\mathbb{Z} \oplus \mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z} \oplus \mathbb{Z}/3\mathbb{Z} \oplus \mathbb{Z}/3\mathbb{Z}\)
• Group B: \(\mathbb{Z}/72\mathbb{Z} \cong \mathbb{Z}/8\mathbb{Z} \oplus \mathbb{Z}/9\mathbb{Z}\)
Even though both have 72 elements, their prime-power "atomic" decompositions (\(4 \times 2 \times 3 \times 3\) vs. \(8 \times 9\)) are different—proving they are fundamentally distinct algebraic structures. In fact, because \(72 = 2^3 \times 3^2\), there are \(3 \times 2 = \mathbf{6}\) possible ways to partition the exponents of \(2\) and \(3\), meaning there exist exactly 6 distinct Abelian groups of size 72 in the entire mathematical universe.
