E4. Smith Normal Form: Algorithmic Decomposition of Abelian Groups

When an Abelian group \(G\) is defined abstractly by \(n\) generators and \(m\) linear relation equations, we store those equations in an \(m \times n\) integer relation matrix \(A\). The Smith Normal Form (SNF) algorithm diagonalizes \(A\) using reversible integer row and column operations (swapping rows/columns, multiplying a row/column by \(-1\), or adding an integer multiple of one row/column to another).

Step-by-Step Concrete Computation

Suppose an Abelian group \(G\) is generated by two elements \(x, y\) subject to two relation equations:
1. \(6x + 6y = 0\)
2. \(6x + 18y = 0\)
We write the coefficients into a \(2 \times 2\) relation matrix:
\(A=\left(\begin{matrix}6&6\\ 6&18\end{matrix}\right)\)
1. Zero Out the First Column Below the Pivot:
Subtract Row 1 from Row 2 (\(R_2 \leftarrow R_2 - R_1\)):
\(\left(\begin{matrix}6&6\\ 0&12\end{matrix}\right)\)
2. Zero Out the First Row to the Right of the Pivot:
Subtract Column 1 from Column 2 (\(C_2 \leftarrow C_2 - C_1\)):
\(\left(\begin{matrix}6&0\\ 0&12\end{matrix}\right)\)
3. Verify the Divisibility Chain (\(d_1 \mid d_2 \mid \dots \mid d_k\)):
In Smith Normal Form, the diagonal entries \(d_1, d_2, \dots\) must divide one another sequentially (\(d_{1}\) divides \(d_{2}\)). Here, \(d_1 = 6\) and \(d_2 = 12\), and since \(6 \mid 12\), the matrix is already in Smith Normal Form! (If \(d_{1}\) did not divide \(d_{2}\), we would add Column 2 to Column 1 and use the Euclidean greatest-common-divisor algorithm to extract \(\gcd(d_1, d_2)\) into the top-left pivot.)
4. Read Off the Group Structure & Prime-Power Atoms:
	• Each diagonal entry \(d_i > 1\) gives a cyclic factor \(\mathbb{Z}/d_i\mathbb{Z}\):
\(G\cong \mathbb{Z}/6\mathbb{Z}\oplus \mathbb{Z}/12\mathbb{Z}\)
	• (Any zero columns at the end would give infinite \(\mathbb{Z}\) factors, and any \(d_i = 1\) gives the trivial group \(\mathbb{Z}/1\mathbb{Z} = \{0\}\), which drops out.)
	• Finally, factoring \(6 = 2 \times 3\) and \(12 = 2^2 \times 3\) decomposes \(G\) into its unique prime-power building blocks:
\(G\cong \mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}\oplus \mathbb{Z}/4\mathbb{Z}\oplus \mathbb{Z}/3\mathbb{Z}\oplus \mathbb{Z}/3\mathbb{Z}\)
(Which turns out to be the exact 72-element group from our previous example!)
